    "title": "The Imperative (Basic)",
    "subtitle": "Commands to tú, usted, nosotros, vosotros and ustedes, affirmative and negative, with pronouns",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · A2 · Verbs · Imperative",
    "next": "Next: A2 · PDF 8 — Object pronouns"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "give commands, instructions and invitations to tú, usted, nosotros, vosotros and ustedes",
        "form affirmative and negative commands, including the 8 irregular tú forms",
        "put object and reflexive pronouns in the right place (dímelo, no me lo digas)",
        "sound polite or friendly with the most common command phrases"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. What the imperative does"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The imperative is used for orders, instructions, advice, requests and invitations: <i>Abre la puerta. Come más verdura. Pasa, por favor.</i> It is used far more in Spanish than in English, and with <i>por favor</i> it is perfectly polite."
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Instructions</b> (recipes, directions): <i>Corta la cebolla y añade sal. Gira a la derecha.</i>",
        "<b>Invitations</b>: <i>Pasa. Siéntate. Come algo.</i>",
        "<b>Suggestions with nosotros</b>: <i>Vamos. Comamos ya. Hablemos mañana.</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Tú: affirmative"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The affirmative tú command is simply the <b>él / ella form of the present tense</b>: <i>(tú) hablas, (él) habla</i>, so the command is <b>habla</b>. The same for <b>come</b> and <b>vive</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Eight common verbs have an irregular tú command. Learn these by heart:"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1,
        2.1
      ],
      "head": [
        "decir",
        "hacer",
        "ir",
        "poner",
        "salir",
        "ser",
        "tener",
        "venir"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "di",
          "haz",
          "ve",
          "pon",
          "sal",
          "sé",
          "ten",
          "ven"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Examples: <i>Dime la verdad. Haz los deberes. Ve a casa. Pon la mesa. Sal de aquí. Sé amable. Ten cuidado. Ven aquí.</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "3. Usted, nosotros, ustedes and all negative commands"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Take the <b>yo</b> form of the present, remove the <b>-o</b> and add the \"opposite vowel\": <b>-ar</b> verbs take <b>-e</b>, <b>-er / -ir</b> verbs take <b>-a</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.6,
        4.2,
        4.2,
        4.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Command to",
        "-ar (hablar)",
        "-er / -ir (comer, vivir)",
        "Used for"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "tú (negative)",
          "-es: no hables",
          "-as: no comas",
          "friend, family"
        ],
        [
          "usted",
          "-e: hable",
          "-a: coma",
          "formal, one person"
        ],
        [
          "nosotros",
          "-emos: hablemos",
          "-amos: comamos",
          "let's ..."
        ],
        [
          "vosotros (negative)",
          "-éis: no habléis",
          "-áis: no comáis",
          "friends, family, group"
        ],
        [
          "ustedes",
          "-en: hablen",
          "-an: coman",
          "formal, group"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "Verbs with irregular yo forms keep that irregularity",
      "text": "Because the command starts from the yo form, <i>hago</i> gives <b>haga, hagas, hagamos, hagáis, hagan</b>; <i>pongo</i> gives <b>ponga</b>; <i>salgo</i> gives <b>salga</b>; <i>tengo</i> gives <b>tenga</b>; <i>vengo</i> gives <b>venga</b>; <i>digo</i> gives <b>diga</b>. Exceptions: <b>ser</b> (sea) and <b>ir</b> (vaya)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Vosotros: affirmative"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Replace the final <b>-r</b> of the infinitive with <b>-d</b>: <b>hablad, comed, vivid</b>, and irregular verbs are regular here too: <b>id, venid, haced, decid, poned, salid, tened</b>. (<i>ser</i> is <b>sed</b>.) With a reflexive pronoun, the d disappears: <b>levantaos</b>, but <b>idos</b> for irse."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Spain vs Latin America",
      "text": "The vosotros command (<i>hablad, comed, venid</i>) exists only where vosotros is used: Spain. In Latin America ustedes is used for every group, so you would hear <b>hablen, coman, vengan</b>. Both are shown in all the tables below."
    },
