    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Negative commands are different",
      "text": "Dutch uses the same verb form: <i>Praat!</i> / <i>Niet praten!</i>. In Spanish a negative tú command cannot use the affirmative form: <b>Habla</b> but <b>No hables</b> (never <i>No habla</i>). The same goes for vosotros: <b>Hablad</b>, but <b>No habléis</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "6. Pronouns and commands"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Affirmative:</b> pronouns attach to the end of the verb. A written accent keeps the stress in place when needed: <i>levántate, siéntese, dímelo, cómpralo</i>. No accent for short forms: <i>dime, hazlo, ponlo</i>.",
        "<b>Negative:</b> pronouns go before the verb, as separate words: <i>no te levantes, no me lo digas, no lo hagas</i>.",
        "<b>With a reflexive verb</b>, the vosotros command loses its d: <i>levantaos</i>, <i>sentaos</i>."
      ]
    },
